4.Learning Approaches in NLP
Supervised Learning

The computer receives examples that already have correct answers. It learns to make similar predictions.

Example: Training a system with labelled messages to recognise fraudulent messages.
Unsupervised Learning
The computer receives information without predefined answers and discovers similarities.
Example: Analysing thousands of news articles and automatically grouping articles about similar subjects.
The main difference is that supervised learning requires target labels, whereas unsupervised learning identifies patterns without them.

5.Converting Language into Numbers

Computers process mathematical information more easily than ordinary human language. NLP therefore transforms text into numerical data.
One common technique is TF-IDF (Term Frequency–Inverse Document Frequency). TF-IDF measures how important a word is within a document compared with its frequency across a collection of documents.
For example, the word "scholarship" may be important in an article about university funding but less important across unrelated articles.
Limitation: TF-IDF does not understand the actual meaning or context of words. Two words with similar meanings may be treated as completely different.

6.Although Somali is widely spoken, developing computer systems for it remains challenging.
Lack of digital resources: There are relatively few large, professionally annotated Somali datasets. This limits the information available for training language models.
Grammatical complexity: Somali words can have different forms depending on grammatical structure. Computers may struggle to recognise that different forms belong to the same root word.
Dialect differences: Somali speakers use different dialects and expressions. A system trained mainly on one variety may misunderstand another.
Possible improvements: Develop digital dictionaries, collect more language samples and involve Somali speakers in building and testing NLP systems.

